    "q-109740": {
      "dom_id": "step-q109740",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Suppose that a matrix $D$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )={\\lambda }^{3}(4-\\lambda )^{2}(7-\\lambda )^{6},$ and for the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0,$ there are exactly $2$ eigenvectors. If a basis of ${R}^{11}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $D,$ how many of these generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ correspond to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0?$",
      "worked_solution": "The number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ that correspond to an eigenvalue $\\lambda $ is equal to the difference between the algebraic multiplicity and the geometric multiplicity of $\\lambda .$\n\nIn the characteristic polynomial\n\n$$\np(\\lambda )={\\lambda }^{3}(4-\\lambda )^{2}(7-\\lambda )^{6},\n$$\n\nwe see that the root $\\lambda =0$ has multiplicity $3.$ So the algebraic multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ is $3.$\n\nHowever, there are only $2$ eigenvectors corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0,$ so the geometric multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ is $2.$\n\nTherefore, the number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ corresponding to $\\lambda =0$ is $3-2=1.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
