    "q-78129": {
      "dom_id": "step-q78129",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Suppose that a matrix $A$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )={\\lambda }^{2}(3-\\lambda )^{4}(5+\\lambda )^{6},$ and for the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-5,$ there are exactly $2$ eigenvectors. If a basis of ${R}^{12}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $A$, how many of these generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ correspond to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-5?$",
      "worked_solution": "The number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ that correspond to an eigenvalue $\\lambda $ is equal to the difference between the algebraic multiplicity and the geometric multiplicity of $\\lambda .$\n\nIn the characteristic polynomial\n\n$$\np(\\lambda )={\\lambda }^{2}(3-\\lambda )^{4}(5+\\lambda )^{6},\n$$\n\nwe see that the root $\\lambda =-5$ has multiplicity $6.$ So the algebraic multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-5$ is $6.$\n\nHowever, there are only $2$ eigenvectors corresponding to  the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-5,$ so the geometric multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-5$ is $2.$\n\nTherefore, the number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-5$ is $6-2=4.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
