    "q-109667": {
      "dom_id": "step-q109667",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Suppose that a matrix $B$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=(4+\\lambda )(\\lambda -5)^{4}(\\lambda +2)^{7},$ and for the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2,$ there are exactly $4$ eigenvectors. If a basis of ${R}^{12}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $B$, how many of these generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ correspond to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2?$",
      "worked_solution": "The number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ that correspond to an eigenvalue $\\lambda $ is equal to the difference between the algebraic multiplicity and the geometric multiplicity of $\\lambda .$\n\nIn the characteristic polynomial\n\n$$\np(\\lambda )=(4+\\lambda )(\\lambda -5)^{4}(\\lambda +2)^{7},\n$$\n\nwe see that the root $\\lambda =-2$ has multiplicity $7.$ So the algebraic multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ is $7.$\n\nHowever, there are only $4$ eigenvectors corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2,$ so the geometric multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ is $4.$\n\nTherefore, the number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-2$ is $7-4=3.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
