    "q-109843": {
      "dom_id": "step-q109843",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "Suppose that a matrix $A$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )={\\lambda }^{2}(\\lambda +1)^{16}(\\lambda -8)^{2},$ the nullity of $(A+I)^{4}$ equals $4,$ and the nullity of $(A+I)^{5}$ equals $7.$ If a basis of ${R}^{20}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $A$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $5$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1?$",
      "worked_solution": "For the given matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $5$ is a vector $v$ such that \n\n$$\n(A+I)^{5}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A+I)^{4}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(A+I)^{4}$ has dimension $4.$ This means that the solutions to $(A+I)^{4}v=0$ form a subspace of dimension $4.$\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(A+I)^{5}$ has dimension $7.$ This means that the solutions to $(A+I)^{5}v=0$ form a subspace of dimension $7.$\n\nNow, since $\\text{Null}(A+I)^{4}$ is a subspace of dimension $4$ in $\\text{Null}(A+I)^{5}$, which is a subspace of dimension $7,$ our basis can contain only $3$ vectors that lie in $\\text{Null}(A+I)^{4}$ but does not lie in $\\text{Null}(A+I)^{5}.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A+I)^{5}-\\text{nullity}(A+I)^{4}=7-4=3\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvectors of rank $5$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
