      "source_example_id": "e-9125",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Number of Generalized Eigenvectors of a Given Rank Using Nullity",
      "kp_id": "e-9125"
    },
    "q-109922": {
      "dom_id": "step-q109922",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "Suppose that a matrix $B$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=(\\lambda +\\sqrt{2})^{3}(\\lambda -2)^{21},$ the rank of $(B-2I)^{6}$ equals $4,$ and the rank of $(B-2I)^{5}$ equals $7.$ If a basis of ${R}^{24}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $B$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $6$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2?$",
      "worked_solution": "For the given matrix $B$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda =2,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $6$ is a vector $v$ such that \n\n$$\n(B-2I)^{6}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(B-2I)^{5}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $B$ must be a $24\\times 24$ matrix.\n\n- Since the rank of $(B-2I)^{5}$ equals $7,$ then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(B-2I)^{5}=24-7=17.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(B-2I)^{5}v=0$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(B-2I)^{5}$ of dimension $17$ in ${R}^{24}.$\n\n- Since the rank of $(B-2I)^{6}$ equals $4,$  then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(B-2I)^{6}=24-4=20.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(B-2I)^{6}v=0$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(B-2I)^{6}$ of dimension $20$ in ${R}^{24}.$\n\nSince $\\text{Null}(B-2I)^{5}$ is a subspace of dimension $17$ in $\\text{Null}(B-2I)^{6}$, which is a subspace of dimension $20,$ our basis can contain only $3$ vectors that lie in $\\text{Null}(B-2I)^{6}$ but do not lie in $\\text{Null}(B-2I)^{5}.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(B-2I)^{6}-\\text{nullity}(B-2I)^{5}=20-17=3\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvectors of rank $6$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
