    "q-109927": {
      "dom_id": "step-q109927",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "Suppose that a matrix $D$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=(\\lambda -7)^{3}(\\lambda +4)^{2}(\\lambda -9)^{9},$ the rank of $(D-9I)^{2}$ equals $11,$ and the rank of $(D-9I)^{3}$ equals $8.$ If a basis of ${R}^{14}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $D$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =9?$",
      "worked_solution": "For the given matrix $D$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda =9,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ is a vector $v$ such that \n\n$$\n(D-9I)^{3}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(D-9I)^{2}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $D$ must be a $14\\times 14$ matrix.\n\n- Since the rank of $(D-9I)^{2}$ equals $11,$ then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(D-9I)^{2}=14-11=3.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(D-9I)^{2}v=0$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(D-9I)^{2}$ of dimension $3$ in ${R}^{14}.$\n\n- Since the rank of $(D-9I)^{3}$ equals $8,$  then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(D-9I)^{3}=14-8=6.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(D-9I)^{3}v=0$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(D-9I)^{3}$ of dimension $6$ in ${R}^{14}.$\n\nSince $\\text{Null}(D-9I)^{2}$ is a subspace of dimension $3$ in $\\text{Null}(D-9I)^{3}$, which is a subspace of dimension $6,$ our basis can contain only $3$ vectors that lie in $\\text{Null}(D-9I)^{3}$ but does not lie in $\\text{Null}(D-9I)^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(D-9I)^{3}-\\text{nullity}(D-9I)^{2}=6-3=3\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvectors of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =9.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
